WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.309 --> 00:00:10.010
Believe it or not

00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:12.140
No matter how powerful an AI chip is,

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:14.136
it can still be hampered by “traffic issues”

00:00:14.141 --> 00:00:14.808
Imagine that

00:00:14.808 --> 00:00:17.611
you have built
the world's most powerful AI processor

00:00:17.611 --> 00:00:18.712
with incredible performance

00:00:18.712 --> 00:00:20.481
But when it tried to transmit data,

00:00:20.481 --> 00:00:22.249
the data was stuck at the output

00:00:22.249 --> 00:00:23.957
because its transmission speed
was slower

00:00:23.957 --> 00:00:25.396
than its computation speed

00:00:25.396 --> 00:00:26.154
It ended up like

00:00:26.154 --> 00:00:28.306
putting a Mercedes engine on bicycle wheels—

00:00:28.306 --> 00:00:30.094
the engine's strength was wasted

00:00:30.094 --> 00:00:33.030
That's why everyone is going crazy
about a new solution—

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:34.331
silicon photonics

00:00:34.331 --> 00:00:36.267
TSMC, NVIDIA, Intel,

00:00:36.267 --> 00:00:38.035
Europe's PhotonDelta,

00:00:38.035 --> 00:00:39.737
and Taiwan's ASE, LuxNet

00:00:39.737 --> 00:00:41.007
are all racing to replace 

00:00:41.007 --> 00:00:43.018
electrical signals with optical ones

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:46.068
They even co-founded
the Silicon Photonics Industry Alliance

00:00:46.068 --> 00:00:49.171
and the Heterogeneously Integrated Silicon Photonics Alliance (HiSPA),

00:00:49.171 --> 00:00:51.151
committing to advancing transmission technology

00:00:51.151 --> 00:00:52.841
in AI computing and data centers

00:00:52.841 --> 00:00:55.321
But what exactly is silicon photonics?

00:00:55.321 --> 00:00:57.272
Can it transform
the semiconductor industry?

00:00:57.272 --> 00:00:59.610
or even challenge
Taiwan's advantage in this field?

00:00:59.610 --> 00:01:02.851
Let's explore this revolution of
light-speed transmission in chips

00:01:02.851 --> 00:01:05.131
because only by
understanding silicon photonics

00:01:05.131 --> 00:01:05.832
can you truly ride

00:01:05.832 --> 00:01:07.434
the wave of the AI era

00:01:10.937 --> 00:01:13.195
To gain the upper hand
in the semiconductor market,

00:01:13.195 --> 00:01:15.575
all semiconductor companies
are going all out

00:01:15.575 --> 00:01:18.701
to develop chips
that are smaller and faster

00:01:18.701 --> 00:01:20.259
In previous episodes, we talked about

00:01:20.259 --> 00:01:22.115
how transistor sizes have shrunk over time

00:01:22.115 --> 00:01:24.006
and how they'll shrink in the future

00:01:24.006 --> 00:01:25.708
But besides transistors,

00:01:25.708 --> 00:01:28.621
another key factor affecting a chip's speed

00:01:28.621 --> 00:01:31.571
is the circuit for signal transmission

00:01:31.571 --> 00:01:32.859
In 1959

00:01:32.859 --> 00:01:36.230
the co-founder of both Fairchild Semiconductor and Intel,

00:01:36.230 --> 00:01:37.702
Dr Robert Noyce,

00:01:37.702 --> 00:01:39.987
proposed a new kind of integrated circuit

00:01:39.987 --> 00:01:42.402
known as the monolithic integrated circuit

00:01:42.413 --> 00:01:44.800
It integrated
every component of a circuit board

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:47.708
—including transistors, resistors, capacitors—

00:01:47.708 --> 00:01:48.984
onto a single chip

00:01:48.984 --> 00:01:49.524
There,

00:01:49.524 --> 00:01:52.479
they were connected by metal wiring
through planar processing

00:01:52.479 --> 00:01:55.805
This is the integrated circuit, or IC,
we often hear about

00:01:55.805 --> 00:01:57.750
Semiconductor manufacturing technologies

00:01:57.750 --> 00:01:59.285
and the IC concept

00:01:59.285 --> 00:02:02.594
allowed us to shrink all components
along with transistors

00:02:02.594 --> 00:02:04.757
As transistors shrink,

00:02:04.757 --> 00:02:06.826
the interconnects between IC components

00:02:06.826 --> 00:02:09.265
must also shrink

00:02:09.265 --> 00:02:11.564
However, the thinner the circuits are,

00:02:11.564 --> 00:02:13.288
the higher their resistance will be

00:02:13.288 --> 00:02:15.779
This will lead to
signal loss and heat issues

00:02:15.779 --> 00:02:16.703
In addition

00:02:16.703 --> 00:02:20.607
the capacitance of the circuits also slows down an IC's response

00:02:20.607 --> 00:02:23.866
Even if IC manufacturing technologies
keep advancing,

00:02:23.866 --> 00:02:26.869
we'll eventually hit the speed limit
for electrical signal transmission

00:02:27.569 --> 00:02:28.236
Moreover,

00:02:28.236 --> 00:02:31.734
the rise of AI and IoT has triggered
exponential growth in

00:02:31.734 --> 00:02:35.324
the demand for fast and large-scale
data transfers between chips

00:02:35.324 --> 00:02:37.740
Companies providing services
based on data processing

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:38.847
such as OpenAI and Google,

00:02:38.847 --> 00:02:39.977
need a data center,

00:02:39.977 --> 00:02:40.476
no

00:02:40.476 --> 00:02:42.232
multiple massive data centers

00:02:42.232 --> 00:02:45.954
to process and store
data from all over the world

00:02:45.954 --> 00:02:47.433
Data traffic in a data center

00:02:47.433 --> 00:02:51.526
can be thousands to millions of times
greater than that at the end-user side

00:02:51.526 --> 00:02:53.506
Accelerating the transmission of
internal data

00:02:53.506 --> 00:02:54.741
is therefore a must

00:02:54.741 --> 00:02:56.832
for high-speed computing in data centers

00:02:56.832 --> 00:02:58.150
To boost transmission speed

00:02:58.150 --> 00:03:01.736
between chips, servers, storage units,
and network equipment,

00:03:01.736 --> 00:03:05.430
electrical signals must
be converted into optical signals

00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:06.466
For the same reason,

00:03:06.466 --> 00:03:08.721
we've already replaced
electrical internet cables

00:03:08.721 --> 00:03:11.185
with fiber-optic ones

00:03:11.185 --> 00:03:14.238
Using light for signal transmission
brings many advantages

00:03:14.238 --> 00:03:15.743
including greater bandwidth

00:03:15.743 --> 00:03:17.812
and longer transmission distances

00:03:17.812 --> 00:03:19.446
In short-range transmissions,

00:03:19.446 --> 00:03:23.484
optical fibers offer more than 10 times
the bandwidth of copper cables

00:03:23.484 --> 00:03:24.367
Moreover,

00:03:24.367 --> 00:03:27.203
optical communication can span
thousands of kilometers

00:03:27.203 --> 00:03:29.475
For example, undersea fiber-optic cables

00:03:29.475 --> 00:03:31.711
have very low signal loss per kilometer

00:03:31.711 --> 00:03:32.793
Given this,

00:03:32.797 --> 00:03:36.231
why not replace all circuits on chips
with optical fibers?

00:03:36.231 --> 00:03:37.984
Why not supercharge transmission speed

00:03:37.984 --> 00:03:39.934
and bandwidth to the max?

00:03:39.934 --> 00:03:42.093
Because in reality, it's not that simple

00:03:42.093 --> 00:03:43.948
A common optical fiber consists of

00:03:43.948 --> 00:03:45.692
a core of transparent materials

00:03:45.692 --> 00:03:47.126
such as glass or plastic,

00:03:47.126 --> 00:03:48.161
and a cladding layer

00:03:48.161 --> 00:03:49.678
of lower refractive index

00:03:49.678 --> 00:03:50.797
This structure enables

00:03:50.797 --> 00:03:52.544
total internal reflection,

00:03:52.544 --> 00:03:54.533
allowing light to travel down the fiber

00:03:54.533 --> 00:03:56.456
even around bends and corners

00:03:56.456 --> 00:04:00.305
This works fine
for long-distance internet cables

00:04:00.305 --> 00:04:04.236
but optical fibers are way too big
to fit on a tiny chip

00:04:04.236 --> 00:04:06.604
So, how we can manipulate light on a silicon chip

00:04:06.604 --> 00:04:09.429
using micrometer-
and sub-micrometer-scale components

00:04:09.429 --> 00:04:11.800
is the central topic
of silicon photonics research

00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:13.216
The good news is

00:04:13.216 --> 00:04:14.254
while silicon absorbs light

00:04:14.254 --> 00:04:17.049
with wavelengths below 1100 nanometers,

00:04:17.049 --> 00:04:19.826
near-infrared light
used in fiber-optic communication

00:04:19.826 --> 00:04:22.629
with wavelengths above 1100 nanometers

00:04:22.629 --> 00:04:25.233
can pass through silicon
without being absorbed

00:04:25.233 --> 00:04:25.698
This means

00:04:25.698 --> 00:04:28.383
for light above 1100 nanometers,

00:04:28.383 --> 00:04:31.052
traveling through silicon
causes no intensity loss

00:04:31.085 --> 00:04:33.321
because of the silicon’s optical properties

00:04:33.321 --> 00:04:34.322
So in principle,

00:04:34.322 --> 00:04:36.624
with existing semiconductor
manufacturing processes

00:04:36.624 --> 00:04:39.125
silicon waveguides
hundreds of nanometers wide

00:04:39.125 --> 00:04:40.747
can be made on a silicon substrate

00:04:40.747 --> 00:04:42.482
Similar to optical fibers,

00:04:42.482 --> 00:04:44.762
waveguides are structures
for light to pass through

00:04:44.762 --> 00:04:45.946
If such optical circuits

00:04:45.946 --> 00:04:48.837
are integrated with electronic ICs
on a chip,

00:04:48.837 --> 00:04:50.757
a smaller package will be required

00:04:50.757 --> 00:04:53.256
and more data can be transferred between chips

00:04:53.256 --> 00:04:55.294
What's currently used
in short-distance optical links

00:04:55.294 --> 00:04:57.379
are the pluggable optical transceiver modules,

00:04:57.379 --> 00:04:58.923
which have been in use for over a decade

00:04:58.923 --> 00:04:59.784
In the module,

00:04:59.784 --> 00:05:01.703
electrical signals from the processor

00:05:01.703 --> 00:05:03.743
are converted into optical signals

00:05:03.743 --> 00:05:06.754
by the transmitter optical sub-assembly

00:05:06.754 --> 00:05:08.717
before going into optical fibers

00:05:08.717 --> 00:05:09.616
At the other end,

00:05:09.616 --> 00:05:12.245
the optical signals
are converted back to electrical ones

00:05:12.245 --> 00:05:14.080
by the receiver optical sub-assembly

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:15.231
Optical transceiver modules

00:05:15.231 --> 00:05:17.483
use traditional laser diodes
as their light sources

00:05:17.483 --> 00:05:18.323
On the receiving end,

00:05:18.323 --> 00:05:21.715
light signals must be converted
back into electrical ones

00:05:21.721 --> 00:05:25.395
before going into
servers or networking devices

00:05:25.395 --> 00:05:27.427
The whole process is like a relay race,

00:05:27.427 --> 00:05:30.261
with the baton passed from runner to runner

00:05:30.261 --> 00:05:31.752
You can probably understand,

00:05:31.752 --> 00:05:34.713
this means there's still room to improve transmission efficiency

00:05:34.713 --> 00:05:37.850
It can't keep up with the explosive data demands of the AI era

00:05:37.850 --> 00:05:40.021
That’s why in terms of signal conversion

00:05:40.021 --> 00:05:41.179
even though silicon photonics

00:05:41.179 --> 00:05:43.304
doesn’t change the fact that electrical-to-optical

00:05:43.304 --> 00:05:45.692
and optical-to-electrical conversions are still needed

00:05:45.692 --> 00:05:46.230
its goal is to

00:05:46.230 --> 00:05:49.673
integrate optical communication components directly onto chips.

00:05:49.673 --> 00:05:53.098
This reduces the need for long-distance electrical interconnections

00:05:53.098 --> 00:05:55.973
dramatically lowering signal delay and power loss.

00:05:55.973 --> 00:05:59.510
It's like swapping congested city roadsfor high-speed bullet trains—

00:05:59.510 --> 00:06:03.314
Data transmission will be
faster, farther, with less loss

00:06:03.314 --> 00:06:04.712
To make this happen,

00:06:04.712 --> 00:06:05.998
new photonic components

00:06:05.998 --> 00:06:07.985
and packaging technologies are needed

00:06:07.985 --> 00:06:09.826
With urgent demand on the rise,

00:06:09.826 --> 00:06:12.572
the development of silicon photonics
is accelerating

00:06:15.790 --> 00:06:17.559
A complete photonic IC

00:06:17.559 --> 00:06:19.985
mainly consists of four components:

00:06:19.985 --> 00:06:21.374
a laser light source,

00:06:21.374 --> 00:06:22.115
a modulator that

00:06:22.115 --> 00:06:24.998
converts electrical signals into optical signals,

00:06:24.998 --> 00:06:26.152
waveguide components

00:06:26.152 --> 00:06:28.443
to transmit and manipulate light signals,

00:06:28.443 --> 00:06:30.523
and a photodetector that converts light signals

00:06:30.523 --> 00:06:32.291
back into electrical ones

00:06:32.291 --> 00:06:32.725
Right now

00:06:32.725 --> 00:06:33.593
researchers aim to use

00:06:33.593 --> 00:06:35.675
mature semiconductor manufacturing processes

00:06:35.675 --> 00:06:38.551
to fabricate and integrate photonic components

00:06:38.551 --> 00:06:40.577
on a single silicon substrate

00:06:40.566 --> 00:06:43.383
and package them with electronic ICs

00:06:43.383 --> 00:06:44.070
However

00:06:44.070 --> 00:06:46.205
they ran into a problem in the very beginning—

00:06:46.205 --> 00:06:47.206
the light source

00:06:47.206 --> 00:06:49.155
A property of monocrystalline silicon is that

00:06:49.155 --> 00:06:51.411
it possesses an indirect bandgap

00:06:51.411 --> 00:06:53.846
So its light-emission efficiency
is extremely low

00:06:53.846 --> 00:06:54.914
Simply put,

00:06:54.914 --> 00:06:56.727
it cannot produce light

00:06:56.727 --> 00:06:58.718
compound and alloy semiconductors

00:06:58.718 --> 00:07:02.488
made from elements adjacent to silicon
on the periodic table

00:07:02.488 --> 00:07:04.772
—groups III and V elements—

00:07:04.772 --> 00:07:06.826
such as gallium arsenide, indium phosphide

00:07:06.826 --> 00:07:08.861
and indium gallium arsenide phosphide

00:07:08.861 --> 00:07:10.463
are direct bandgap materials

00:07:10.463 --> 00:07:13.341
They are ideal
for making semiconductor lasers

00:07:13.341 --> 00:07:15.201
So researchers have to incorporate

00:07:15.201 --> 00:07:17.737
III-V materials or pre-made laser diodes

00:07:17.737 --> 00:07:20.273
onto monocrystalline silicon substrates

00:07:20.273 --> 00:07:23.376
through bonding or flip-chip processes

00:07:23.376 --> 00:07:25.945
This is known as heterogeneous integration

00:07:25.945 --> 00:07:27.897
There are several methods to do it

00:07:27.897 --> 00:07:30.149
One is flip-chip bonding,

00:07:30.149 --> 00:07:32.084
a method in which electrodes are made first

00:07:32.084 --> 00:07:33.621
on a laser diode chip

00:07:33.621 --> 00:07:35.788
Then the chip is flipped and bonded

00:07:35.788 --> 00:07:37.757
By soldering or direct metal bonding,

00:07:37.757 --> 00:07:39.425
it is connected to the electrical contacts

00:07:39.425 --> 00:07:40.693
of a photonic chip

00:07:40.693 --> 00:07:43.596
This creates a structure called
the "direct-attached laser,"

00:07:43.963 --> 00:07:45.565
and this method is called

00:07:45.565 --> 00:07:47.099
"hybrid integration"

00:07:47.099 --> 00:07:48.324
While cost-effective,

00:07:48.324 --> 00:07:50.937
it's harder to precisely align the laser diode

00:07:50.937 --> 00:07:52.939
with the center of the silicon waveguide

00:07:52.939 --> 00:07:54.006
Another common method

00:07:54.006 --> 00:07:55.942
begins with building waveguide components

00:07:55.942 --> 00:07:57.577
on a silicon wafer,

00:07:57.577 --> 00:08:02.418
then bond the III-V material
directly to a photonic chip

00:08:02.418 --> 00:08:03.449
After that

00:08:03.449 --> 00:08:06.819
through etching, metallization, and passivation,

00:08:06.819 --> 00:08:09.755
the III-V materials are made into
laser diodes

00:08:09.789 --> 00:08:10.723
This product is called

00:08:10.723 --> 00:08:12.358
the "heterogeneous integrated laser,"

00:08:12.358 --> 00:08:14.958
or the "heterogeneous Si photonic laser"

00:08:14.958 --> 00:08:18.264
The III-V materials
are bonded with the chip first

00:08:18.264 --> 00:08:20.800
Then, the laser diodes
are aligned with waveguides

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:23.391
through semiconductor manufacturing processes

00:08:23.391 --> 00:08:25.166
Compared with flip-chip bonding,

00:08:25.166 --> 00:08:26.839
this avoids the trouble of picking

00:08:26.839 --> 00:08:28.600
placing, and aligning laser chips

00:08:28.600 --> 00:08:31.205
when integrating laser diodes and silicon chips

00:08:31.205 --> 00:08:33.379
In addition to these,
one proposed method suggests

00:08:33.379 --> 00:08:35.681
by first growing a virtual germanium substrate

00:08:35.681 --> 00:08:36.549
on silicon

00:08:36.549 --> 00:08:38.818
then layering
a germanium-tin alloy on top

00:08:38.818 --> 00:08:41.329
Allegedly, this can also make laser diodes

00:08:41.329 --> 00:08:42.622
Some researchers believe

00:08:42.622 --> 00:08:45.078
these integration processes
are too troublesome

00:08:45.078 --> 00:08:46.525
Why not just place the laser diode

00:08:46.525 --> 00:08:48.260
outside the photonic chip

00:08:48.260 --> 00:08:50.928
This would also avoid
chip overheating issues

00:08:50.928 --> 00:08:53.199
Although each method of integrating laser diodes

00:08:53.199 --> 00:08:54.433
with photonic chips

00:08:54.433 --> 00:08:57.036
offers different advantages
for different applications,

00:08:57.036 --> 00:08:59.438
heterogeneous integration
through wafer bonding

00:08:59.438 --> 00:09:00.506
is currently considered

00:09:00.506 --> 00:09:02.942
the most widely adopted and promising solution

00:09:02.942 --> 00:09:05.526
for large-scale integration

00:09:05.526 --> 00:09:07.046
Besides the light source,

00:09:07.046 --> 00:09:09.874
another major challenge
in silicon photonics development

00:09:09.874 --> 00:09:13.161
is converting electrical signals
into optical signals

00:09:13.161 --> 00:09:14.153
Simply put,

00:09:14.153 --> 00:09:16.521
to send information via light,

00:09:16.521 --> 00:09:18.190
one should be able to use the “on” 

00:09:18.190 --> 00:09:22.036
and “off” states of light to represent high and low voltages

00:09:22.036 --> 00:09:24.096
You might think this sounds easy

00:09:24.096 --> 00:09:26.510
Isn't it just turning on the laser
at high voltage,

00:09:26.510 --> 00:09:28.634
and turning it off at low?

00:09:28.634 --> 00:09:29.835
Let's put it this way,

00:09:29.835 --> 00:09:32.044
a direct modulated laser like this

00:09:32.044 --> 00:09:33.956
has some inherent disadvantages

00:09:33.956 --> 00:09:36.042
For example,
being directly modulated by current

00:09:36.042 --> 00:09:37.944
causes a stronger photothermal effect,

00:09:37.944 --> 00:09:40.112
wavelength shift, and other issues

00:09:40.112 --> 00:09:42.715
And while the bandwidth of directly modulated lasers

00:09:42.715 --> 00:09:44.783
can run over 10 GHz,

00:09:44.783 --> 00:09:48.688
it's still lower than
that of external modulators

00:09:48.688 --> 00:09:50.790
So what's an external modulator?

00:09:50.790 --> 00:09:52.733
First, some context:

00:09:52.733 --> 00:09:55.506
A laser diode emits a continuous light wave

00:09:55.506 --> 00:09:58.688
when biased with a constant current
above its threshold

00:09:58.688 --> 00:10:01.334
An external modulator is an optical modulator

00:10:01.334 --> 00:10:04.842
that comes after a laser diode and
is controlled by an electrical signal

00:10:04.842 --> 00:10:07.273
It in turn controls
the light intensity in a waveguide,

00:10:07.273 --> 00:10:10.009
which means it controls
the “on” and “off” states of light

00:10:10.009 --> 00:10:12.519
This is the optical modulator
currently used in photonic ICs

00:10:12.519 --> 00:10:13.822
and they basically work like this

00:10:13.822 --> 00:10:15.980
In intrinsic silicon semiconductors

00:10:15.980 --> 00:10:16.983
—pure silicon—

00:10:16.983 --> 00:10:19.783
a change in the concentrations of
electrons and holes

00:10:19.783 --> 00:10:22.661
cause slight variations
in the refractive index

00:10:22.661 --> 00:10:24.690
If a piece of pure silicon
is sandwiched

00:10:24.690 --> 00:10:27.665
between a p-type and
an n-type semiconductor,

00:10:27.665 --> 00:10:29.428
applying a voltage across the ends

00:10:29.428 --> 00:10:32.071
can alter
the carrier concentration in the middle,

00:10:33.389 --> 00:10:36.344
thereby changing its refractive index

00:10:36.344 --> 00:10:37.436
But how does that help

00:10:37.436 --> 00:10:41.240
switch a laser beam “on” and “off”
within a waveguide?

00:10:42.628 --> 00:10:45.502
That's where
the microring resonator comes in

00:10:45.502 --> 00:10:48.014
—also known as the “ring resonator”

00:10:48.014 --> 00:10:49.949
Although such a ring-shaped waveguide

00:10:49.949 --> 00:10:52.447
isn't physically connected
to a straight waveguide,

00:10:52.447 --> 00:10:54.548
its ability to resonate with the light

00:10:54.548 --> 00:10:55.788
determines whether the light

00:10:55.788 --> 00:10:58.824
can pass through the straight waveguide

00:10:58.891 --> 00:10:59.845
You see,

00:10:59.845 --> 00:11:02.628
A ring resonator
has a certain resonance wavelength

00:11:02.628 --> 00:11:03.963
When it is tuned to match

00:11:03.963 --> 00:11:06.701
the wavelength of
the light in a straight waveguide,

00:11:08.271 --> 00:11:11.303
a traveling wave resonance
forms in the ring,

00:11:11.303 --> 00:11:13.873
thereby transferring energy from the straight waveguide

00:11:13.873 --> 00:11:16.108
to the ring through coupling

00:11:16.108 --> 00:11:17.243
As a result

00:11:17.243 --> 00:11:19.278
no light remains in the straight waveguide

00:11:19.278 --> 00:11:21.714
This corresponds to an “off” state

00:11:21.714 --> 00:11:24.050
When the ring's wavelength
doesn't match the light's,

00:11:24.050 --> 00:11:25.484
no resonance occurs,

00:11:25.484 --> 00:11:27.975
and the energy remains in the straight waveguide

00:11:27.975 --> 00:11:30.084
This corresponds to an “on” state

00:11:30.084 --> 00:11:31.387
Now, remember that

00:11:31.387 --> 00:11:33.900
the carrier concentration in the ring waveguide

00:11:33.900 --> 00:11:36.935
changes its refractive index,

00:11:36.935 --> 00:11:39.265
thereby tuning its resonance wavelength

00:11:39.615 --> 00:11:42.654
So there's no need to change
the laser's wavelength

00:11:42.654 --> 00:11:46.105
Instead, by putting
a p-type and an n-type semiconductor

00:11:46.105 --> 00:11:47.740
on each side of a ring waveguide,

00:11:47.740 --> 00:11:49.981
we can tweak its resonance wavelength

00:11:49.981 --> 00:11:52.393
and modulate continuous light

00:11:52.393 --> 00:11:53.245
The ring resonator

00:11:53.245 --> 00:11:57.136
enables high-frequency modulation
in a space of tens of microns

00:11:57.871 --> 00:11:59.285
In 2010

00:11:59.285 --> 00:12:00.717
Intel used this design

00:12:00.717 --> 00:12:02.621
along with the hybrid silicon laser

00:12:02.621 --> 00:12:04.557
to launch a transceiver module

00:12:04.557 --> 00:12:06.992
with a transmission speed of 50 gigabits

00:12:06.992 --> 00:12:09.595
—or 50 billion bits—per second

00:12:09.595 --> 00:12:12.331
It marked the commercial debut
of silicon photonics,

00:12:12.331 --> 00:12:13.929
but that was more than a decade ago

00:12:13.929 --> 00:12:14.366
So

00:12:14.366 --> 00:12:18.003
how has silicon photonics developed
since 2010?

00:12:21.507 --> 00:12:23.743
Many years ago,
Intel set its sights on

00:12:23.743 --> 00:12:26.112
the massive data demands inside data centers

00:12:26.112 --> 00:12:29.262
So it started investing in
silicon photonics R&amp;D and integration

00:12:29.262 --> 00:12:31.617
To this day,
it remains a leader in the field

00:12:31.617 --> 00:12:32.918
In June 2024,

00:12:32.918 --> 00:12:34.587
Intel introduced its first

00:12:34.587 --> 00:12:36.522
based on the silicon photonics technology,

00:12:36.522 --> 00:12:39.658
fully integrated
optical compute interconnect chiplet

00:12:39.658 --> 00:12:41.961
co-packaged with an Intel CPU

00:12:41.961 --> 00:12:44.214
It supports transmission using 8 wavelengths

00:12:44.214 --> 00:12:45.965
on a single waveguide or fiber

00:12:45.965 --> 00:12:47.233
Its bidirectional data transfer

00:12:47.233 --> 00:12:50.127
achieves an astonishing speed of 4 terabits

00:12:50.127 --> 00:12:52.104
—or 4 trillion bits—per second

00:12:52.104 --> 00:12:54.774
Other major US players such as
Cisco, Broadcom, and IBM,

00:12:54.774 --> 00:12:57.009
are also racing to catch up,

00:12:57.009 --> 00:12:57.676
not to mention

00:12:57.676 --> 00:12:59.411
the numerous startups and design houses

00:12:59.411 --> 00:13:01.247
who all want a slice of the pie

00:13:01.780 --> 00:13:03.249
What about Taiwan?

00:13:03.249 --> 00:13:04.316
While Taiwan continues to lead

00:13:04.316 --> 00:13:06.252
in advanced electronic IC manufacturing,

00:13:06.252 --> 00:13:07.787
making photonic ICs doesn't require

00:13:07.787 --> 00:13:10.422
cutting-edge nodes
such as 3nm or 5nm,

00:13:10.422 --> 00:13:12.725
because photonic components
are significantly larger

00:13:13.025 --> 00:13:15.261
So Taiwan doesn't have a clear advantage here

00:13:15.261 --> 00:13:17.771
But companies like TSMC and ASE

00:13:17.771 --> 00:13:20.432
have recognized
the rise of silicon photonics

00:13:20.432 --> 00:13:21.934
In recent years, they've introduced

00:13:21.934 --> 00:13:24.103
relevant integration and packaging solutions

00:13:24.103 --> 00:13:25.254
In March 2025,

00:13:25.254 --> 00:13:25.938
NVIDIA launched

00:13:25.938 --> 00:13:27.740
a set of silicon photonics networking switches

00:13:27.740 --> 00:13:30.165
that use
TSMC's COUPE packaging technology

00:13:30.165 --> 00:13:32.211
—Compact Universal Photonic Engine

00:13:32.211 --> 00:13:34.713
Each port supports up to
16 terabits per second

00:13:34.713 --> 00:13:36.448
Via different port configurations such as

00:13:36.448 --> 00:13:39.351
512 ports of 800 Gbps

00:13:39.351 --> 00:13:42.587
or 2048 ports of 200 Gbps,

00:13:42.587 --> 00:13:45.424
enabling an aggregate throughput of 400 Tbps

00:13:45.424 --> 00:13:48.981
This enables AI factories
to connect millions of GPUs across sites

00:13:48.981 --> 00:13:51.497
Moreover, TSMC's COUPE uses its own

00:13:51.497 --> 00:13:53.483
SoIC-X packaging technology

00:13:53.483 --> 00:13:55.968
to stack electronic ICs on photonic ICs

00:13:55.968 --> 00:13:58.879
for maximum power efficiency
through vertical integration

00:13:58.879 --> 00:14:01.106
In addition,
packaging giant ASE has introduced

00:14:01.106 --> 00:14:02.907
the Fan-Out-Package-on-Package technology

00:14:02.907 --> 00:14:05.753
as a new solution for
silicon photonics packaging

00:14:05.753 --> 00:14:07.313
Although “silicon photonics” is a term

00:14:07.313 --> 00:14:09.393
which has made headlines in tech news
in recent years,

00:14:09.393 --> 00:14:12.719
the underlying technologies
have taken decades to develop

00:14:12.719 --> 00:14:13.552
In the future

00:14:13.552 --> 00:14:16.433
it could become as pivotal to technological advancement

00:14:16.433 --> 00:14:17.782
as the transistor today

00:14:17.782 --> 00:14:18.557
As mentioned

00:14:18.557 --> 00:14:20.392
Taiwanese companies continue to push forward

00:14:20.392 --> 00:14:22.062
not only in silicon photonics,

00:14:22.062 --> 00:14:24.463
but in every field of
the semiconductor industry

00:14:24.463 --> 00:14:26.165
From smaller process nodes

00:14:26.165 --> 00:14:28.133
to brand new memory structures,

00:14:28.133 --> 00:14:29.501
the semiconductor industry

00:14:29.501 --> 00:14:31.837
requires constant innovation

00:14:32.491 --> 00:14:34.887
As we enter the AI era
and look back on history,

00:14:34.887 --> 00:14:36.041
it is clear that technology

00:14:36.041 --> 00:14:37.096
wouldn't be where it is today

00:14:37.096 --> 00:14:38.755
without the vision of Babbage,

00:14:38.769 --> 00:14:39.970
the persistence of Shockley,

00:14:40.003 --> 00:14:41.989
the boldness of the Traitorous Eight,

00:14:41.989 --> 00:14:43.265
the leadership of Noyce,

00:14:43.265 --> 00:14:45.290
the ambition of Morris Chang,

00:14:45.290 --> 00:14:47.425
and the relentless efforts of countless others

00:14:47.425 --> 00:14:49.294
Taiwan's silicon shield

00:14:49.294 --> 00:14:52.330
is the result of generations of efforts

00:14:52.631 --> 00:14:53.632
For Taiwan,

00:14:53.632 --> 00:14:55.734
the semiconductor industry
isn't just a sector—

00:14:55.734 --> 00:14:57.936
it's the backbone of
the nation’s strength

00:14:57.936 --> 00:15:00.171
That's why it took us 14 episodes

00:15:00.205 --> 00:15:03.275
to trace the roots and rise
of the semiconductor industry

00:15:03.708 --> 00:15:04.887
In the future,

00:15:04.887 --> 00:15:06.868
what breakthroughs lie ahead?

00:15:06.868 --> 00:15:08.947
What technologies will emerge?

00:15:08.951 --> 00:15:12.287
Whose names will join
the silicon hall of fame?

00:15:12.755 --> 00:15:14.356
Hey, it could be yours

00:15:14.390 --> 00:15:16.425
Don't sell yourself short

00:15:16.425 --> 00:15:19.995
If you're curious about
the R&amp;D of semiconductors,

00:15:19.995 --> 00:15:21.754
please check out PanSci's channel

00:15:21.754 --> 00:15:22.421
and subscribe

00:15:22.421 --> 00:15:24.682
for more deep dives into the future of tech

00:15:25.499 --> 00:15:27.550
This video series will end here,

00:15:27.550 --> 00:15:30.854
but the race for semiconductors
is only just beginning

00:15:30.854 --> 00:15:31.821
Thanks for watching

00:15:31.821 --> 00:15:32.956
See you next time

